{"summary":"Jason Ku introduces an algorithms course that he will teach with Eric Domain and Justin Solomon. The course focuses on solving computational problems, proving correctness, analyzing efficiency, and communicating solutions clearly—often through writing. He defines a problem as a relation between possible inputs and correct outputs, typically described with a predicate that checks whether an output is valid. The class will study general algorithms that work on inputs of arbitrary size, rather than one fixed instance.","chapters":[{"title":"Course introduction and goals","start":"00:12"},{"title":"What counts as a computational problem","start":"02:59"},{"title":"Problems as input-output relations","start":"04:06"},{"title":"Predicates and the birthday example","start":"05:30"},{"title":"General problems and algorithms","start":"07:28"}],"keyPoints":["The course emphasizes solving problems, proving correctness, arguing efficiency, and communicating ideas.","Students should expect substantial writing, not just coding.","A computational problem relates possible inputs to one or more correct outputs.","Predicates can specify how to check whether an output is correct for a given input.","Algorithms should apply to arbitrarily large inputs, not only a fixed classroom-sized instance.","An algorithm is a fixed-size procedure that takes an input and produces a correct output."]}